 "transcript": {
  "answer": "**Perth's electricity prices aren't rising faster than elsewhere. They're rising more slowly than almost anywhere else.**\n\n**Electricity CPI, annual change to August 2026 (ABS)**\n\n| City | Annual change |\n|---|---|\n| Perth | **+2.4%** |\n| Brisbane | +5.9% |\n| Sydney | +13.2% |\n| Australia | +13.2% |\n| Melbourne | +16.8% |\n| Adelaide | +21.4% |\n\nPerth's electricity price has been moving by about +2.4% to +2.5% a year since April 2026. WA household tariffs are regulated, so they change in small, predictable steps.\n\n**Why it can still feel huge**\n- **Rebates may be distorting the picture.** Perth's electricity figures swung wildly between mid-2024 and early 2026. For example, they showed +289% in Aug 2025 and Feb 2026, then −50% in early 2025. Those swings are too large to be real tariff changes. I think they come from government energy bill credits being applied and then ending, which changes what households actually pay. I haven't confirmed this against the ABS notes. If you got a credit earlier and it has stopped, your bill would jump even though the price per unit barely moved.\n- **Overall costs are rising faster in Perth.** All-groups CPI in Perth was +4.3% in the year to August 2026, against +4.0% nationally. Other costs may be squeezing your budget more than power is.\n- **The CPI measures prices, not bills.** Your bill depends on how much you use. Air-conditioning, a pool, an EV or a hot spell can all push it up. Your tariff and any rebates also affect it.\n\nTo work out whether your own bill is unusual, compare your usage in kWh and your daily rate with the same period last year. If the rate hasn't changed much but the total has, usage or a lapsed credit is the likely cause.",
